# Runner host — copy to `.env` at the deploy checkout root and fill in.
#
#   cp deploy/host/.env.example .env
#
# This machine runs untrusted repository code. Every credential here is scoped
# to one door on purpose; do not reuse the broker secret as an enrollment or
# tunnel credential.
#
# **Do not commit the filled-in file.**

# ─── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# This stack's compose project — the prefix on every container, image and
# network it creates, and therefore what keeps two deployments on one machine
# apart. Required rather than defaulted, because sharing it is silent: the
# second `up` does not refuse, it adopts the first stack's containers and
# recreates them with this file's configuration.
#
#   freeinference-cloud-agent-sandbox-staging
#   freeinference-cloud-agent-sandbox-prod
COMPOSE_PROJECT=

# The other environment's stack, when this machine runs both. Comma-separated.
# The deploy's stray-stack guard reports containers of this shape belonging to
# projects it does not know; a named sibling is expected company rather than
# the hand-made duplicate the guard exists to catch.
#
# **Setting this makes the host-state root below required.** Two stacks sharing
# it share the enrolment credential, so the second registers as the first host
# whatever AGENT_RUNNER_HOST says. Chat files never live in a host directory;
# each group owns one Docker container writable layer.
SIBLING_COMPOSE_PROJECTS=

# This stack's own state on the machine. Optional on a host running one
# environment, required once SIBLING_COMPOSE_PROJECTS is set:
#
#   /var/lib/freeinference-cloud-agent-staging/host
#   /var/lib/freeinference-cloud-agent-prod/host
AGENT_HOST_STATE_ROOT=

# The sandbox image this host runs. Required rather than defaulted: a
# defaulted image name is how a host silently runs something else. Tag it per
# environment, so a rebuild on one side cannot replace the other's image:
#
#   freeinference-cloud-agent-job:staging
#   freeinference-cloud-agent-job:prod
AGENT_SANDBOX_IMAGE=

# Serves workspace files and terminals. This is a broker-only secret; host
# registration, heartbeat, and claim use the enrollment-issued credential.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
AGENT_WORKSPACE_BROKER_TOKEN=

# ─── The three tunnels ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Three destinations, three permissions, three keys, each with its own account
# on the far host. Restrict each key there with command="" plus permitopen —
# a shell there is not part of what a runner host needs, and a key that opens
# two of these makes the separation cosmetic. Only the control-plane key also
# gets permitlisten, for its one reverse listener; the account Match blocks in
# deploy/host/sshd-tunnels.conf.example refuse the other two any remote
# forward. Admin → Servers → Server setup generates all of this.

# 1. The gateway (model calls). Reached by the sandbox and the runner.
AGENT_TUNNEL_SSH_DESTINATION=
AGENT_TUNNEL_SSH_KEY=
AGENT_TUNNEL_SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS=

# 2. The control plane's MCP relay (tools), port 8001. Reached by the sandbox.
#    Never 8000: that listener also serves jobs, sessions and admin.
AGENT_MCP_TUNNEL_SSH_DESTINATION=
AGENT_MCP_TUNNEL_SSH_KEY=
AGENT_MCP_TUNNEL_SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS=

# 3. The control plane itself (claim, register, heartbeat), port 8000.
#    Reached by the runner and **not** by the sandbox — it is deliberately
#    absent from the relay network, so no group bridge is ever given it.
AGENT_CONTROL_PLANE_TUNNEL_SSH_DESTINATION=
AGENT_CONTROL_PLANE_TUNNEL_SSH_KEY=
AGENT_CONTROL_PLANE_TUNNEL_SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS=

# 3b. The way back, over that same connection: the control plane reaching this
#     host's workspace broker. Every file listing, terminal keystroke and git
#     status goes control plane → broker, and on a remote runner host there is
#     otherwise no route for it — this machine can open a connection to the
#     control plane's, and that one cannot open one back.
#
#     All three are required by the supported standalone topology. Even on the
#     same physical machine, its control-plane Compose stack is on a different
#     network and cannot resolve `workspace-broker`; setting the advertise URL
#     without the tunnel reports an address nothing answers on, and every
#     broker call 503s.
#
#     BIND must be the *control plane host's* docker bridge gateway — the
#     address its containers use to reach their own host. Find it with:
#
#         docker network inspect <the control plane's network> \
#           --format '{{range .IPAM.Config}}{{.Gateway}}{{end}}'
#
#     Not 127.0.0.1 (invisible to those containers) and not 0.0.0.0 (publishes
#     the broker to that machine's whole network behind one shared token).
#     A non-loopback bind needs `GatewayPorts clientspecified` for the control
#     account in the control plane host's sshd, plus a PermitListen and the
#     key's permitlisten for exactly this address and port; see
#     deploy/host/sshd-tunnels.conf.example. Never `yes`, which silently binds
#     the wildcard address instead. Also firewall the port on that machine so
#     it answers only on its Docker bridge interface (docs/operations.md).
AGENT_BROKER_REVERSE_BIND=
AGENT_BROKER_REVERSE_PORT=
#     What the runner reports at enrolment, stored on its host row and dialled
#     by the control plane. The far end of the forward above.
AGENT_WORKSPACE_BROKER_ADVERTISE_URL=

# ─── Pool membership (required by shared retained execution) ─────────────

# A one-time token, spent at first start for a lasting per-host credential.
# Once that exists the runner claims with it, and the control plane reads the
# host from the credential rather than from the request body — which is what
# makes drain, pin and revoke per-host decisions rather than suggestions.
#
# Issued by an admin and bound to this host's name. It may be blank after the
# first successful registration because the returned credential is persisted
# under AGENT_HOST_STATE_ROOT.
AGENT_ENROLL_TOKEN=

# Enrolled execution environment ID, shared by this stack's runner replicas.
# Defaults to the host's name. Multiple stacks in the same control-plane pool
# need distinct IDs, credentials and broker addresses, even on one server.
AGENT_RUNNER_HOST=

# Physical server inventory name, shared by all execution environments on it.
# sandbox_host.sh defaults this to the host's hostname. Set explicitly when
# invoking Compose directly. Never use a container hostname or a runtime suffix.
# This is display metadata; AGENT_RUNNER_HOST remains the enrolled deployment ID.
AGENT_PHYSICAL_HOST=

# Turns this machine runs at once until an admin sets a number on the Servers
# page; the runner grows or shrinks its executor pool to match at runtime.
# Shared forked chats need at least two.
AGENT_HOST_SLOTS=3

# The host-state root configured near the top also holds the enrollment lock
# and deployment-SHA readiness marker. These are host infrastructure metadata,
# never chat files or Agent CLI state. An enrollment token is single-use, so a
# credential that dies with the container needs a new token every restart. The
# deploy scripts manage the SHA marker; do not add AGENT_DEPLOYMENT_SHA here.

# ─── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Bounded wait used by both parallel deployment halves. On a true cold start,
# the host can finish building before the control API/MCP relay is listening;
# it waits rather than publishing readiness or failing a one-shot race.
SHARED_READY_TIMEOUT=900
# Exact daemon-side DockerRootDir reported by `docker info`. Leave this at
# /var/lib/docker. Put a durable disk under that path by bind-mounting
# <disk>/docker onto /var/lib/docker and <disk>/containerd onto
# /var/lib/containerd. A custom Docker data-root is rejected.
# This path is never bind-mounted into a sandbox or broker container.
AGENT_RETAINED_DOCKER_DATA_ROOT=/var/lib/docker
# The next three, AGENT_TIMEOUT_S and the AGENT_TERMINAL_* limits can also be
# set on the admin Settings page, or for one server on the Servers page; a value
# set there wins over this file until it is cleared. See ENVIRONMENT.md.
# Immutable per-container pool of member UIDs/private tmpfs slots (2..128).
AGENT_SHARED_RESIDENT_CAPACITY=8
# Container size for new groups; admin Settings and per-server overrides take
# over on heartbeat. All sessions and forks in a group share these resources.
AGENT_SANDBOX_CPU_MILLICORES=2000
AGENT_SANDBOX_MEMORY_MIB=4096
# 0 means no per-container quota. A positive MiB limit requires Docker overlay2
# backed by XFS with pquota/prjquota; unsupported hosts reject group creation.
AGENT_SANDBOX_DISK_MIB=0
# Time after the latest settled turn (and last terminal close) before the
# Session Supervisor issues docker stop. The stopped container is retained;
# this is not the one-turn execution timeout and does not delete chat state.
AGENT_CHAT_IDLE_TIMEOUT_S=1800
# Largest repository checkout a first turn may import, in MiB (1..65536). It
# counts the files and the fetched commit's Git objects, which store most
# content a second time, so a repository with 600 MB of files can need more
# than 1024. Past it the turn fails before the agent starts; a retry fails the
# same way.
AGENT_SOURCE_IMPORT_MAX_MIB=1024
# Fallback grace period for direct, out-of-band Docker Compose operations.
# deploy/host/sandbox_host.sh activate calls Engine stop with an infinite
# timeout: the runner stops claiming on SIGTERM and exits only after every turn
# in hand, so a supported deployment never kills user work.
AGENT_RUNNER_STOP_GRACE=5m
AGENT_SANDBOX_BACKEND=kata
# Host capacity sampling: the machine's CPU, memory and retained-container
# count, shown per server on the admin page and kept as a 24-hour history.
# The compose file mounts the host's /proc/stat, /proc/meminfo and boot id
# read-only for it. Set to 0 only on a host where those mounts are absent or
# unwanted: the runner then reports no capacity, and the page shows the server
# as not measured instead of guessing.
AGENT_HOST_CAPACITY_ENABLED=1
# Which storage probe measures the filesystem holding the sandbox writable
# layers — the disk whose fullness the Servers page shows. `overlay_root` (the
# default) reads the runner's own root filesystem; OverlayFS makes its statfs
# describe the upper layer's filesystem, which is the same disk the sandboxes
# write to. The host script attests that topology against its own df of the
# mount root and writes `.storage-probe/verdict.json`; without a matching entry the
# runner reports "unverified" rather than a number, because a project quota and
# a small disk are indistinguishable from inside the container. Set to `off` to
# stop measuring storage on this host (the rollback): the page then explains
# the gap and every other metric keeps working. `off` needs an `up` to reach
# replicas that are already running, because a container's environment is fixed
# when it is created. These two spellings are the only ones accepted; anything
# else is refused when the host is checked.
AGENT_HOST_STORAGE_PROBE=overlay_root
AGENT_GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=
AGENT_GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=

# ─── Sandbox network (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One network, and nothing to choose. Each sandbox group gets its own routable
# bridge, and before anything joins one the runner and broker install this
# deployment's host nftables table. It admits public IPv4 on TCP 80/443, the
# resolver below on TCP/UDP 53, and the two relays' service ports; it refuses
# this host, private and special-purpose addresses, other groups, other ports,
# UDP 443 and IPv6. Setup scripts and the agent share the same bridge.
#
# RFC1918 pool cut into one /29 per active group. It must not overlap any other
# deployment on this machine — of two whose pools overlap, the one installed
# later refuses — nor any network this host already routes (LAN, VPN, private
# services): the runner reads the host's routes and refuses a pool that shares
# addresses with one. /16 permits 8192 simultaneous groups; idle groups
# release theirs.
#
#   staging: 10.240.0.0/16
#   prod:    10.241.0.0/16
AGENT_EGRESS_CHAT_NETWORK_POOL=
# The one IPv4 resolver sandboxes may use, on TCP and UDP 53. A public resolver
# works anywhere. A site resolver on a private address, or on this host, works
# too and becomes the one private address a sandbox can reach, on port 53 only.
# Not loopback: a sandbox cannot reach this host's 127.0.0.53.
#
#   AGENT_EGRESS_DNS_SERVER=1.1.1.1
AGENT_EGRESS_DNS_SERVER=
# Extra IPv4 blocks to refuse, comma-separated, on top of the built-in private
# and special-purpose ones — an institution's services on public addresses, for
# example. It can only add protection.
AGENT_EGRESS_PROTECTED_CIDRS=

# ─── Agent CLI versions (build time) ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# Extra versions the image also carries, comma-separated — the *menu* an admin
# can switch this deployment between from the admin page, with no rebuild.
#
#   CLAUDE_CODE_EXTRA_VERSIONS=2.1.235
#
# Blank is the normal case: the image then holds exactly the source-reviewed
# defaults and the admin page offers one option per runtime. Build time decides
# the menu, the admin page decides which item is active — the control plane
# cannot serve a version this image does not contain. The defaults themselves
# are shared protocol pins; update the Dockerfile and
# `SHARED_SANDBOX_RUNTIME_PINS` together rather than overriding one host.
#
# Costs disk: roughly 300 MB per extra claude-code against a ~3.2 GB image.
#
# **An extra is a version the event parser must handle.** `runtimes.py`
# normalizes one shape per runtime; putting a version here is a claim that it
# parses. Run `pytest -m external tests/external/test_runtime_stream_conformance.py`
# against it first — offering a version nobody checked is how a broken option
# reaches an operator's dropdown.
CLAUDE_CODE_EXTRA_VERSIONS=
CODEX_EXTRA_VERSIONS=
PI_EXTRA_VERSIONS=
OPENCODE_EXTRA_VERSIONS=
KILO_EXTRA_VERSIONS=

# Admin measurement control is available by default; no profiling variables
# are required. The runner and broker collect only for selected operations.
# These optional overrides are passed to both containers.
AGENT_OPERATION_PROFILING_ENABLED=false
AGENT_OPERATION_PROFILING_RUN=
AGENT_OPERATION_PROFILING_SINK=
# Blank defaults to Admin control unless legacy ENABLED=true selects a static
# experiment. `disabled` forbids collection; `environment` uses only ENABLED/RUN.
AGENT_OPERATION_PROFILING_MODE=
